On 8/8/13 9:56 PM, Marcus Brubaker wrote:
> We should expose the mdivide_left_spd function which is what is really needed here.  Not sure why it's not exposed right
> now, I probably forgot to finish the user visible parts.

Before exposing this, can you explain the naming convention _spd?

   Are there restrictions on the arguments?

   Is it an approximation to mdivide_lft of some kind?

If not, why would someone ever use mdivide_left?

And is there a corresponding mdivide_right_spd?

To expose the function, we want the function itself to be
unit tested, the function signature exposed to the parser,
the include in the top-level include, unit tests for models
using all instantations of the functions with double and var,
and descriptions in the manual.  Can you make a pull request
for this, Marcus?  If not, I can do it once I understand the
answers to the above so that I can doc it.  There's some doc
on the wiki.

> That said, the code Ben provided might actually be a bit slower, depending on the size of Sigma and how many
> observations there are.  cholesky_decompose and inverse do not have explicit derivatives, so they fall back to the (much
> slower) Eigen code paths.
>
> I would guess that:
>
> Sigma_chol <- cholesky_decompose(Sigma);
> for (a in 1:100) x[a] ~ multi_normal_chol(mu,Sigma_chol);

I think the message cutoff before the punchline for this.

At the risk of sounding like a mini-Andrew, I'd say we should put this
kind of discussion in the manual!  Ben and Marcus are the obvious
candidates for writing this --- I don't think I could manage.

- Bob

