I agree with Marcus.  I think that partitioning the factors of the model can be important, but this partitioning can be done in different ways.  A hierarchical model, for example, can be partitioned in more than one way.

For posterior predictive checks, the key point is that the user must decide what aspects of the model will persist and what aspects will be replicated.  This really has do be done as part of a graphical model structure, although I suspect there's a way to hack it in the current version of Stan.  But it goes deeper than prior/likelihood/posterior.

A

