That would be the most general.  But having methods (even if they're wrappers)

prior { return lp_prior + Jacobian correction }
likelihood { return lp_likelihood + Jacobian correction }
posterior { return lp_prior + lp_posterior + Jacobian correction }

would be my ideal use case.

Again, posterior is not necessary equal to prior + likelihood.  The idea would be to either have sub-blocks

model {
  prior{
  }
  likelihood {
  }
}

or three different blocks

model {}
prior {}
likelihood {}

with posterior = model + prior + likelihood so that the prior/likelihood terms are completely optional and would only be used if the user had decomposition in mind.  Bonus points for being able to use the model/prior/likelihood functions in the generated quantities block.

My original motivation was that adding new blocks would just amount to duplicated the existing model parsing code.

