Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I'm all for merging and working from there to make
everyone's life easier.   But let's not ignore anyone's
objections by outvoting them!  Having said that, let's also
not wait forever.  How's that for wishy-washy, namby-pamby,
pass-the-buck, weasel-worded advice?

You might want to rebase it all first, as it looked
like there were a lot of broken intermediate commits.

- Bob

On 8/28/13 12:25 PM, Daniel Lee wrote:
> I have one vote for merge from Michael. (I'm counting the other two as rejections.)
>
> I'm voting merge. I'll wait until Bob, Jiqiang, or Ben weigh in on this. One more merge vote will get it in.
>
>
> On Wed, Aug 28, 2013 at 12:21 PM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com <mailto:betan...@gmail.com>> wrote:
>
>     Merge merge merge.  :-)
>

>     On Aug 28, 2013, at 6:19 PM, Daniel Lee <bea...@alum.mit.edu <mailto:bea...@alum.mit.edu>> wrote:
>
>>     Yes, wait until post-merge.
>>
>>     Does anyone object to the merge as-is? It passes tests. https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/pull/172
>>
>>
>>
>>
>>     On Wed, Aug 28, 2013 at 12:03 PM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com <mailto:betan...@gmail.com>> wrote:
>>
>>         I see - adaptation is indeed off but the step size gets initialized no matter what.  The fix is easy - just
>>         move the try blocks in the two sampler init functions to the init adapt function.
>>
>>         Should we wait to fix until after the arg_refactor merge?  I'll also add details on the dual averaging and HMC
>>         options into the doc.  Note that it'll be a few day (no wireless on my laptop and not an Ethernet port in sight).
>>

>>         On Aug 28, 2013, at 4:24 PM, Jiqiang Guo <guo...@gmail.com <mailto:guo...@gmail.com>> wrote:
>>
>>>         Running an example indicates something else; the stepsize is 0.0625.
>>>
>>>         $ ./dogs data=dogs.data.R sample algorithm=hmc engine=static adapt engaged=0
>>>
>>>         $ more samples.csv
>>>         # stan_version_major = 1
>>>         # stan_version_minor = 3
>>>         # stan_version_patch = 0
>>>         # model = dogs
>>>         # method = sample (Default)
>>>         #   sample
>>>         #     num_samples = 1000 (Default)
>>>         #     num_warmup = 1000 (Default)
>>>         #     save_warmup = 0 (Default)
>>>         #     thin = 1 (Default)
>>>         #     adapt
>>>         #       engaged = 0
>>>         ....
>>>         #     algorithm = hmc (Default)
>>>         #       hmc
>>>         #         engine = static
>>>         #           static
>>>         #             int_time = 6.2831853071795898 (Default)
>>>         #         metric = diag_e (Default)
>>>         #         stepsize = 1 (Default)
>>>         ....
>>>         # output
>>>         #   file = samples.csv (Default)
>>>         #   append_sample = 0 (Default)
>>>         #   diagnostic_file =  (Default)
>>>         #   append_diagnostic = 0 (Default)
>>>         #   refresh = 100 (Default)
>>>         lp__,accept_stat__,stepsize__,int_time__,alpha,beta,A,B
>>>         -4640.98,0,0.0625,6.28319,-0.67269,-4.93523,0.510334,0.00718883
>>>         -4640.98,0,0.0625,6.28319,-0.67269,-4.93523,0.510334,0.00718883
>>>
>>>
>>>         On Wed, Aug 28, 2013 at 7:48 AM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com <mailto:betan...@gmail.com>> wrote:
>>>
>>>             It rounds to the nearest integer (by static casting from a double to an int).  In this case you would get
>>>             6 steps.
>>>
>>>             On Aug 28, 2013, at 1:33 PM, Jiqiang Guo <guo...@gmail.com <mailto:guo...@gmail.com>> wrote:
>>>
>>>>             I rephrase my question.  What are the actual values of int_time and stepsize if I turn off adaptation
>>>>             for static HMC? They cannot be 2 *pi and 1 at the same time since int_time/stepsize is not an integer
>>>>             (assuming my understanding is correct).
>>>>
>>>>             --
>>>>             Jiqiang
>>>>
>>>>

>>>>             On Wed, Aug 28, 2013 at 3:09 AM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com <mailto:betan...@gmail.com>>

>>>>             wrote:
>>>>
>>>>                 These are optimal values for IID Gaussians, which is e best default I can think of.
>>>>
>>>>                 On Aug 28, 2013, at 3:21 AM, Jiqiang Guo <guo...@gmail.com <mailto:guo...@gmail.com>> wrote:
>>>>
>>>>>                 Thanks a lot.  One question about int_time below.
>>>>>
>>>>>                 --
>>>>>                 Jiqiang
>>>>>
>>>>>                 On Tue, Aug 27, 2013 at 4:20 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com <mailto:ca...@alias-i.com>> wrote:
>>>>>
>>>>>
>>>>>                         Last, I do not know what is "int_time" for HMC.
>>>>>
>>>>>
>>>>>                     That's what we used to call step size.  The reason for the new, more
>>>>>                     explanatory terminology is that the step size is just the time
>>>>>                     interval for the discretization of the Hamiltonian simulation.  The
>>>>>                     negative log probability is the potential energy, we provide a random
>>>>>                     initial kinetic energy each iteration, and then we trace out the path
>>>>>                     given the potential and kinetic energies given the mass matrix.  The
>>>>>                     int_time is the time interval for each step of the discrete simulation.
>>>>>
>>>>>
>>>>>                 From the help (below), int_time is the total integration time and we have
>>>>>                 an argument called stepsize.
>>>>>
>>>>>                               int_time=<double>
>>>>>                                 Total integration time for Hamiltonian evolution
>>>>>                                 Valid values: 0 < int_time
>>>>>                                 Defaults to 2 * pi
>>>>>
>>>>>                 So can I understand int_time = stepsize * (number of leapfrog steps)?  If yes, I think
>>>>>                 the default values for int_time (2 * pi) and stepsize = 1 are not good for each other.
>>>>>
>>>>>
>>>>>
